[Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  the structure of octopus traveler  presents a huge risk by telling its  story with 8 different characters and  just as many plot lines the RPG has  inevitable peaks and valleys in its  narrative flow the spotlight shifts  between revenge stories rescue missions  and coming-of-age tales at a moment's  notice  it's a lot to balance so going into the  game I had high expectations for the  writing not just the separate stories  unfolding but the presentation and  dialogue tying them all together the  narrative glue so to speak without this  octopus might feel like a collection of  short stories rather than the cohesive  adventure of a multicultural group now  60 hours into my playthrough and  numerous chapters behind me I'm shocked  to find that narrative glue not an  octopus writing but in its combat  right from the outset octopus travelers  combat promises depth no matter which of  the eight characters you start with you  soon discover two of the more impactful  mechanics at play boost points and enemy  shields the former are octa paths  offensive fuel every turn each of your  character is a cruise one boost point  saving these points allows your party to  unleash extra physical attacks or  amplified magical abilities  deciding when to unleash your boost  points becomes a pillar of octa Pat's  combat and the fulcrum of your tactical  decisions this is due in large part to  enemy shields each opponent has a number  of armor layers ranging anywhere from  one to more than ten in some cases foes  can increase their shield rating  throughout the duration of the fight  making it increasingly difficult to  penetrate their armor by discovering an  enemy's weaknesses to certain weapon  types or elemental attacks you can lower  their shield rating with each successive  strike if you manage to deplete an  enemy's shielding entirely you break  them for the remainder of this turn and  the next leaving them open to increased  damage in the meantime when combined  with boost points the act of breaking an  enemy becomes the heart of your  decision-making should you spend  multiple points to stun that enemy with  an upcoming turn or should you save  boost for the next round when you can  unleash it on an already defenseless foe  weighing the benefits of HP damage  versus shield depletion becomes critical  throughout the game if you played many  of this type of RPG before or octopops  gameplay inspirations are probably  fairly obvious stunning enemies is  reminiscent of the persona and Shin  Megami Tensei games while boost points  harken back to bravely default and  bravely second a series that shares two  producers with octopus traveler but  octopus combat is truly impressive  because of its transparency it doesn't  hide much information at all and it  doesn't leave much of the fighting up to  chance yes you'll occasionally miss an  attack because of your low accuracy or  an opponent's high evasion but outside  of these scenarios you'll rarely be  surprised you observe the next turns  attack order and make informed decisions  based on your boost points the enemy's  shields and the characters in your party  it's like playing a game of poker in  which every player's hands is showing  defeat is usually a result of  carelessness or the enemy just having  better cards this isn't to say that  there isn't variety and surprise to be  found in octopus combat in fact they're  abundant there is a slew of variables at  play support abilities job skills and  weapon effects to name a few you can  swap secondary jobs right from the start  menu  buffing each character's passive bonuses  to prepare for the next boss fight this  isn't to mention each character's unique  powers such as Han it's be steaming or  elephant's knowledge of concoction  there are also advanced jobs that  further improve your travelers you begin  your quest as a scholar or a hunter or a  merchant but as the hours roll by and  you face an ever-expanding roster of  enemies your characters evolve into  walking Arsenal's  your characters develop not just through  the writing but the combat as well that  right there is the major reason I grew  to love octopus travelers combat over  the course of my playthrough I have a  tendency to look at games like  individual parts the writing the  soundtrack the art direction but octopus  traveller like my favorite games finds  ways to make those variables work in  concert it tells its story not just  through dialogue and exposition but  through its gameplay as well in other  words the traditional written aspects  only tell half of the story Hana is a  hunter because the developers told us so  but also because she can bide her time  in battles waiting for the perfect  moment to strike and deal massive  amounts of damage elephant's plotline  revolves around helping the sick and his  combat abilities reflect that he removes  party elements he erases status effects  and sometimes in the right circumstances  against the right enemies he weaponized  is the very diseases he usually works to  remove after all he who Kuras can make  it fill story elements like these are  littered throughout octopus combat we  learned cyrus is a scholar because of  his origin story but we believe it  because of the way he studies his  opponents highlighting their weaknesses  and sharing them with his allies his  entire story is centered on the belief  that knowledge should be shared and not  hoarded for one's own personal gain for  the combat to drive that home is  thrilling to see I could keep going down  this road highlighting each character's  combat abilities to reinforce my point  but I think you get it octa bath  traveler is a long game especially if  you complete all four chapters of each  character's narrative and while many of  the quests begin on pretty boring notes  many of them blossom into truly engaging  territory stories involving ethical  dilemmas and personal crises for each of  the travelers but on many levels I think  the game's creators knew they needed  strong gameplay to get you to those  interesting narrative twists they knew  you'd spend the vast majority of your  time fighting so why not tell their  story there in the heat of pitched  battles octa bath travelers combat  that holiest of storytelling mantras  show don't tell as I said before or  octopus eight plot structure runs the  risk of feeling wobbly or disjointed but  its combat has the legs to pull you  through to tell you a story on its own  it defines these characters with verbs  rather than adjectives looking back on  my time with the JRPG I don't see eight  individual quests but one unifying  narrative conveyed not just in what the  developers are telling us but what we  ourselves are doing and I think that's  something future JRPGs should continue  to strive for  you

